// SL-224: a coroutine-transform rejection raised while lowering an IMPORTED
// body anchors in the module that DEFINES that body, not in the entry module.
//
// Design 74 (A8) gave `CoroTransformError` a `source_file` so a rejection could
// be anchored like a type error; 21 of the 48 raise sites never passed one, and
// `sawc.py`'s surfacing site then rendered the span against the ENTRY module's
// reporter. That is the nastiest kind of diagnostic bug: the LINE and COLUMN
// are the defining module's while the FILE is the entry module's, so the reader
// is shown a real-looking `file:line:col` carrying a snippet of unrelated code
// — and the span MOVES whenever an unrelated file changes length. Construction
// is the funnel now (see `CoroTransformError`), and `_FrameBuilder._error`
// stamps `self.src_file` for every in-builder site, so the file cannot be
// forgotten one site at a time.
//
// The oracle is the FILE, and the line/column deliberately do NOT discriminate:
// before the fix this same program reported the same span against THIS file,
// where that line is a comment. Right span, wrong file, absurd snippet — which
// is why only the path can tell the two compilers apart.
//
// THE VEHICLE CHANGED with design 275 U3: the refusal this used to ride — a
// suspension inside a `for` over a non-range iterable — became a SPLIT
// (SL-317). The closure-body refusal (SL-316) is the shape table's other REFUSE
// row and is raised through the same `_FrameBuilder._error`, so it asks the
// same question of the same funnel.
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: a closure body is not driven
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: sl224_anchor.saw:16:12

import modules.sl224_anchor

func run() -> Int {
    let v = sl224_anchor.spin([1, 2])
    v
}

func main() {
    print(__saw_drive(run()))
}
